pySMESH: NETGENPlugin runs inside a host process: no working-directory change, no file,
no redirect of std::cout, and netgen silent at the source (NETGENPlugin V9_16_0; applies
after NETGENPlugin_local_size_by_subshape.patch).

Why: pySMESH is embedded in a host application, and every NETGEN compute broke the host's
process state:
* NETGENPlugin_NetgenLibWrapper changed the process's working directory into a new
  temporary directory for its whole life (ChdirRAII, SALOMEDS_Tool::GetTmpDir), and never
  removed that directory.
* It created a second temporary directory for the netgen log, and pointed std::cout at
  that log for the whole process (setOutputFile); KEEP_NETGEN_OUTPUT left netgen's
  progress on the console instead (129 lines for one cylinder at message level 3).
* ReadErrors read "test.out" from the working directory, and RemoveTmpFiles deleted
  "test.out", "problemfaces" and "occmesh.rep" from it: in the host's own working
  directory, once the chdir is gone. With netgen 6.2.2101 test.out was never written
  anyway (ngcore initialises testout to a null stream, and the wrapper opened the file
  only when testout was null), so ReadErrors found nothing.
* SALOME_NETGEN_DISABLE_MULTITHREADING overrode the hypothesis's thread count.
SALOMEDS_Tool (the temporary-directory helpers) is also part of SALOME's study layer,
which pySMESH does not build.

Change:
* NETGENPlugin_Mesher.hxx: ChdirRAII and the SALOMEDS_Tool include are gone. The wrapper
  keeps netgen's debug stream in memory (_testout) and the stream it replaced; the log
  file members and functions (setOutputFile, getOutputFileName, removeOutputFile,
  RemoveTmpFiles) are gone. New static TestOutput() returns the debug text of the live
  wrapper.
* NETGENPlugin_Mesher.cxx: the wrapper sets netgen's message level to 0, points netgen's
  message streams (mycout, myerr: PrintMessage, PrintWarning, PrintError) at a stream
  with no buffer, turns ngcore's logger (std::clog) off, and points netgen::testout at
  its own memory stream, restored in the destructor. ReadErrors parses that text with the
  same cursor rules as SMESH_File (TextCursor), so the bad edges and the intersecting
  triangles that netgen reports still reach the compute error. SetDefaultParameters no
  longer reads SALOME_NETGEN_DISABLE_MULTITHREADING. The SMESH_File and SALOMEDS_Tool
  includes are gone.

The netgen globals that the wrapper sets are private to the netgen copy that pySMESH links
statically into _core.pyd; the binding serializes every NETGEN compute with one
process-wide lock. A mesh does not change: only output and files do.

